# November 21, 2026: Utah, a first statewide scatter. The state had 17 places, all round Tintic and the West Desert
# Pony Express stations; this batch spreads 18 more from Cache Valley and Promontory to St. George, Bluff and Flaming Gorge.
# Facts come from the linked sources, opened in this session: Wikipedia (read with tools/wiki_raw.py), National Register
# forms read as text from NPGallery (Frisco Charcoal Kilns 1982, Fort Deseret 1970), the pages of whoever runs each place
# (Utah State Parks, the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints' historic-site pages, the Forest Service, the NPS, the
# Hole in the Rock Foundation, the Topaz Museum, Union Station Ogden, the Grafton Heritage Partnership), the Utah Geological
# Survey, Preservation Utah, Utah Humanities, the Utah State Historic Preservation Office and HMdb.org.
# Hours come from those pages as read on the batch date; where none could be read the entry says so.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
def L(t,u): return [t,u]
BATCH={'key':'ut_pbty','label':'Utah: a first scatter across the state, from Promontory to Bluff','added':'2026-11-21 05:12'}
LDSCOVE=L('Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints: Cove Fort (hours)','https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/learn/locations/cove-fort')
LDSBY=L('Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints: Brigham Young Winter Home and Office (hours)','https://www.churchofjesuschrist.org/learn/historic-sites/utah/brigham-young-winter-home?lang=eng')
PLACES=[
# ---- north ----
N('big-fill-promontory','Big Fill and Big Trestle abutments, Golden Spike','Promontory','ut','rail','traces',41.64188,-112.47943,'site','public','walk','','1869',
  'On the east slope of the Promontory Mountains the Central Pacific and Union Pacific, racing each other in 1869, built two grades side by side through the hardest ground on this stretch of the transcontinental railroad. The Central Pacific crossed a ravine on a huge earth fill, the Big Fill; the Union Pacific threw up a wooden trestle, the Big Trestle, beside it. Everything was dug and carried by hand; the NPS says the walk you take today took the workers about three months to build.',
  'A 1.5-mile loop that goes out on the original Central Pacific grade and back on the Union Pacific grade: the Big Fill, the stone abutments that held the Big Trestle, rock cuts and drill marks from blasting, and stretches of graded but never-finished line. Numbered markers along the trail.',
  'Two railroads grading past each other for 250 miles, and the fill and trestle footings they left side by side.',
  'Golden Spike National Historical Park (NPS). The trail starts from a lot on the park road east of the visitor center. The NPS says the park\'s outdoor features are open every day, sunrise to sunset, year-round; winter weather can close some areas. Leave artifacts where they lie. Park entrance fee not checked.',
  [L('NPS: Big Fill Loop Trail','https://www.nps.gov/thingstodo/big-fill-loop-trail.htm'),
   L('NPS: Golden Spike operating hours and seasons','https://nps.gov/gosp/planyourvisit/hours.htm')],
  th=[41.63728,-112.49168],thn='Big Fill trail parking (park road)',brick='no',hours='Grounds sunrise to sunset daily; visitor center March to October daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., November to March Thursday to Monday 9 to 5'),
N('logan-tabernacle','Logan Tabernacle','Logan','ut','worship','standing',41.73232,-111.83406,'exact','view','drive','','1864–1891',
  'A Latter-day Saint tabernacle whose foundation was begun in 1864. Work stopped twice, the second time in 1877 when the builders left to finish the nearby Logan Temple; the assembly hall was finished in 1881 and the building dedicated in 1891. It is still used for stake conferences, graduations, concerts and lectures.',
  'Walls of local quartzite from Green Canyon, with white limestone corners and trim. Inside, Wikipedia describes pine balcony pillars painted to look like marble and a Pilcher pipe organ, rebuilt in 1952–53 and again later. Whether the hall opens to visitors outside events: interior not checked.',
  '',
  'An active church building in downtown Logan; seen from the street. No page of the church\'s own giving visiting hours was read; hours not checked.',
  [W('Logan Tabernacle')],brick='no'),
N('ogden-union-station','Union Station, Ogden','Ogden','ut','rail','standing',41.22083,-111.97972,'exact','open','drive','stations','1924',
  'Ogden became the junction of the Union Pacific and Central Pacific because Promontory was too remote. The present station, in the Spanish Colonial Revival style, was finished in 1924 on the foundation of the earlier one after a stone from the old clock tower fell on a railroad clerk. Passenger service faded after 1950; Ogden City took the building in 1977 and opened it as museums in 1978.',
  'The 1924 station at the west end of Historic 25th Street, with the Utah State Railroad Museum, the Browning firearms museum, a classic car museum, a cowboy and western heritage museum and art galleries inside. Wikipedia says the hall\'s trusses, once painted in bright patterns, now carry a faux wood grain, and that a steam derrick and rotary snow plow were given to the museum.',
  '',
  'Owned by Ogden City. 2501 Wall Avenue. Union Station\'s own page gives museum admission ($5 adults, less for seniors, students and children) and free parking on the east side of the building; the page carries notices from several years back, so ring (801) 393-9890 before a trip.',
  [W('Union Station (Ogden, Utah)'),L('Union Station: admission, hours, location','https://theunionstation.org/main/ticketstours/')],
  brick='?',hours='Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Christmas and New Year\'s Day'),
N('cottonwood-paper-mill','Cottonwood Paper Mill (the Old Mill)','Cottonwood Heights','ut','industry','ruins',40.62708,-111.79986,'exact','view','drive','burned','1883',
  'The Deseret News built this stone paper mill at the mouth of Big Cottonwood Canyon in 1883, under Henry Grow. It was sold to the Granite Paper Mills Company in 1892, and on April 1, 1893 its stock of paper caught fire and left only a stone shell. In 1927 it was partly rebuilt as an open-air dance hall, the Old Mill Club, which ran into the 1940s.',
  'The stone shell of the mill. Preservation Utah listed it among its most endangered places in 2025: the owner since about 1987, a development company, applied in 2024 to demolish it; the city refused that route in November 2024, but in January 2026 its future was still unsettled. Check that it still stands before a trip.',
  '',
  'Private, fenced. Preservation Utah says it cannot be entered but can be seen from Big Cottonwood Canyon Road (6851 South). The parking point is a lot in Old Mill Park, which OpenStreetMap and the federal land map show as a public park beside it.',
  [W('Cottonwood Paper Mill'),L('Preservation Utah: The Old Mill (2025 most endangered)','https://www.preservationutah.org/2025-most-endangered-1/the-old-mill')],
  th=[40.62928,-111.80270],thn='Old Mill Park lot, Big Cottonwood Canyon Road',brick='no'),
N('iosepa-cemetery','Iosepa townsite and cemetery','Skull Valley','ut','town','traces',40.54207,-112.73387,'site','public','drive','cemeteries','1889–1917',
  'Iosepa was a settlement of Latter-day Saint converts from Hawaii and other Pacific islands, who were not welcome in Salt Lake City hotels and restaurants. The church-owned Iosepa Agriculture and Stock Company bought a ranch in Skull Valley, and the first 46 settlers arrived on August 28, 1889; in 1911 it won the state prize for the best-kept town. When the Laie Hawaii Temple was built most went home, and by January 1917 Iosepa was empty.',
  'The fenced cemetery with its headstones and a monument with a bronze bust of a Polynesian warrior, a few foundations and a lone fire hydrant from the townsite, which the marker puts half a mile southwest. A Memorial Day gathering is held here every year.',
  'A Pacific Islander town in the Utah desert.',
  'The HMdb marker says it is reached from Utah Route 196 at milepost 23 in Skull Valley. Wikipedia says the Iosepa Historical Association looks after the site and that visitors and campers are welcome; who owns the ground was not found in a source read. Remote: no services named.',
  [W('Iosepa, Utah'),L('HMdb: Iosepa Settlement Cemetery marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=301559')],brick='?'),
N('swett-ranch','Swett Ranch','Dutch John','ut','landmark','standing',40.878049,-109.48817,'site','open','drive','','1909–1967',
  'Oscar Swett\'s family homesteaded on a bench of the Uinta Mountains near Red Canyon in 1909 and ran the ranch for 58 years, mostly by horse and hand power. A 14-acre part with nine buildings and three structures was put on the National Register in 1979; a corral built in 1905, before the homestead, is largely original.',
  'The family\'s cabins and farm buildings, with a self-guided tour in summer; the Forest Service calls it an example of pioneer ranching with little modern machinery. Which buildings open inside: interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Ashley National Forest (Flaming Gorge National Recreation Area). From Vernal take US 191 north about 39 miles, then Forest Road 158 for 1.3 miles; vault toilet by the parking lot, picnic tables, no drinking water (Forest Service page).',
  [W('Swett Ranch'),L('Forest Service: Swett Ranch Historical Homestead','https://www.fs.usda.gov/r04/ashley/recreation/swett-ranch-historical-homestead')],
  brick='?',hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day, daily 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. (self tours)'),
# ---- central ----
N('thistle-ghost-town','Thistle','Thistle','ut','town','ruins',39.99266,-111.49755,'area','view','drive','drowned','1878–1983',
  'A railroad town in Spanish Fork Canyon that serviced Denver and Rio Grande Western trains from the 1880s. In April 1983 a huge earthflow dammed the Spanish Fork River and the lake behind it drowned the town; state and federal agencies called it the costliest landslide in United States history, and it brought Utah\'s first presidential disaster declaration. The highway and the railroad were rebuilt higher up.',
  'Wikipedia says only a few structures were left partly standing, and that they can be seen from a view area on US 89 or from the California Zephyr. The Utah Geological Survey describes the slide and its scar from a pullout on the highway; the old rail and road grades show below the new ones.',
  'A town drowned by a landslide in 1983.',
  'The remains are on land not on the federal public-land map; owner not found in a source read. Look from the US 89 pullout. The parking point is a lot OpenStreetMap maps on US 89 about 1.5 km south; that it is the view area Wikipedia names is not confirmed.',
  [W('Thistle, Utah'),L('Utah Geological Survey: GeoSights, Thistle landslide revisited','https://geology.utah.gov/map-pub/survey-notes/geosights/thistle-landslide/')],
  th=[39.97966,-111.50321],thn='US 89 pullout',brick='?'),
N('fort-deseret','Fort Deseret','Deseret','ut','fort','ruins',39.26464,-112.65444,'site','public','drive','forts','1865',
  'Settlers built this 550-foot square adobe fort in 1865, during the Black Hawk War, when the Army was tied up by the Civil War. The walls were mud and straw on lava-rock footings, about 10 feet high, with bastions at two corners. When Black Hawk came to Deseret in 1866 demanding cattle, the fort let the settlers bargain for peace.',
  'Eroded adobe walls, mostly along the east side, at the bastions and at the corners; the 1970 Register form gave them as 6 to 7 feet high and badly worn, with layers of mud and straw showing in the fallen parts. The state historic preservation office has run volunteer days to stabilise the walls with beams. It calls it the only surviving adobe fort of the kind once common in Utah.',
  'The last of Utah\'s adobe forts, melting back into the desert.',
  'Fort Deseret State Park on the federal land map; the 1970 Register form says Utah State Parks owned it. Just south of Deseret. No current managing page was read: hours and rules not checked.',
  [W('Fort Deseret'),L('National Register nomination: Fort Deseret (1970, NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/70000624_text'),
   L('Utah SHPO: Fort Deseret remudding project','https://ushpo.utah.gov/event/fort-deseret-remudding-project/')],brick='no'),
N('topaz-incarceration-site','Topaz incarceration camp site','Delta','ut','fort','traces',39.41755,-112.7791,'site','public','drive','','1942–1945',
  'Topaz, the Central Utah Relocation Center, was one of ten camps where the United States government held Japanese Americans and Japanese immigrants during the Second World War, most of them from the West Coast. It opened on September 11, 1942 and closed on October 31, 1945; at its peak about 8,100 people lived in its tarpaper barracks, making it for a time one of Utah\'s largest towns. By 2017 the Topaz Museum Board had bought 634 of the 640 acres of the central site.',
  'Wikipedia says many foundations, concrete-lined pits and other ground-level traces can be seen, but few buildings remain and brush has taken over. A monument placed by the Japanese American Citizens League in 1976 stands at the northwest corner of the central area. The Topaz Museum, with the camp\'s history and objects, is in Delta.',
  '',
  'Privately owned and protected by the Topaz Museum, which asks visitors to read its rules, drive only on the roads, take nothing, and see the museum in Delta (55 West Main) first. The site is about 16 miles northwest of Delta at 10750 West 4500 North. The museum is open Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. (museum\'s own page); a BLM page gives other hours (11 to 5, closed Sundays) and is older.',
  [W('Topaz War Relocation Center'),L('Topaz Museum: Topaz site','https://topazmuseum.org/'),L('Topaz Museum: the museum','https://topazmuseum.org/museum/'),
   L('BLM: Topaz Internment Camp','https://blm.gov/visit/topaz-internment-camp')],brick='?'),
N('territorial-statehouse-fillmore','Territorial Statehouse','Fillmore','ut','civic','standing',38.9683,-112.32577,'exact','open','drive','','1852–1855',
  'Fillmore was chosen as the capital of Utah Territory for its central place, and the statehouse was begun in 1852 to a plan of four wings under a dome. Only the south wing was built before the money ran out. The legislature held its one full session here in December 1855, and the seat of government went back to Salt Lake City in 1858. It is Utah\'s oldest standing government building.',
  'The red stone south wing, now a state park museum; Wikipedia says the masons carved their initials into some outside stones because they expected the walls to be stuccoed over. On the grounds are restored pioneer cabins and an 1867 stone schoolhouse.',
  'The capitol that got one wing.',
  'Territorial Statehouse State Park Museum (Utah State Parks), in Fillmore; parking on site. Day-use fee on the park\'s page (amount not checked). Phone 435-743-5316.',
  [W('Utah Territorial Statehouse'),L('Utah State Parks: Territorial Statehouse State Park Museum','https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/territorial-statehouse/')],
  brick='no',hours='Monday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day'),
N('cove-fort','Cove Fort','Cove Fort','ut','fort','standing',38.60167,-112.58028,'site','open','drive','forts','1867',
  'Ira Hinckley built the fort in 1867 for Brigham Young as a way station on the road between Fillmore and Beaver, during the Black Hawk War. It is a square 100 feet on a side, of black volcanic rock and dark limestone, with walls 18 feet high. It served travellers, the Pony Express and the telegraph until the early 1880s; it was never attacked. A fire had burned the north rooms by 1903; the church later bought and restored it.',
  'The restored stone fort with its rooms set out as the Hinckleys\' way station, shown by guided tour; Ira Hinckley\'s cabin moved here from Coalville, and a visitor center.',
  '',
  'Run by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints as a historic site open to the public; near the junction of I-15 and I-70. Phone +1-435-819-0647; the church\'s page asks visitors to confirm by phone.',
  [W('Cove Fort'),LDSCOVE],brick='no',hours='Monday to Saturday 11 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday noon to 5; closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day'),
N('fruita-schoolhouse','Fruita Schoolhouse','Fruita','ut','civic','standing',38.28811,-111.24818,'exact','public','drive','','1896–1941',
  'The one-room log school of the Mormon farming village of Fruita, now inside Capitol Reef National Park. It was used from 1896 to 1941, for 8 to 22 pupils from first to eighth grade, and also served as the village meeting house and church. The interior was plastered in 1935; the NPS restored it to its 1930s look in 1989.',
  'The log schoolhouse, furnished inside; the NPS says visitors may look through the windows. Fruita\'s orchards and the Gifford farmhouse are nearby.',
  '',
  'Capitol Reef National Park (NPS), on SR 24; a small lot beside it (OpenStreetMap). Leave no trace, and do not add to the old inscriptions. Park fees not checked.',
  [L('NPS: Fruita Schoolhouse','https://www.nps.gov/places/fruita-schoolhouse.htm'),
   L('NPS: The Fruita Schoolhouse (history)','https://www.nps.gov/care/learn/historyculture/fruitaschoolhouse.htm'),W('Fruita Rural Historic District')],
  brick='no'),
# ---- east ----
N('sego-ghost-town','Sego','Thompson Springs','ut','town','ruins',39.03354,-109.70288,'site','view','drive','','1908–1955',
  'Henry Ballard found coal in this Book Cliffs canyon in 1908. Under the American Fuel Company and then the Chesterfield Coal Company the camp, renamed Neslen and then Sego, grew into one of Grand County\'s main towns, with a five-mile spur railroad down to Thompson. The mine was unprofitable by 1947; a fire in 1949 and floods in the 1950s finished it. Much of it was listed on the National Register in 2017 as the Ballard-Sego Coal Mine Historic District.',
  'The stone company store, foundations, dugouts and the remains of a few houses. The two-storey wooden boarding house collapsed between 2009 and 2010. Wikipedia says an underground coal fire has smouldered for decades, and the old rail grade, with a few trestles, follows the canyon road. Keep out of mine openings.',
  'A coal town in a slot of the Book Cliffs.',
  'About 5 miles north of Thompson Springs up Sego Canyon. Who owns the townsite was not found in a source read, so look from the canyon road; the BLM rock-art site lower in the canyon is public.',
  [W('Sego, Utah'),L('Utah Humanities: Ghost Towns: Sego, a coal town with a colorful past','https://stories.utahhumanities.org/item/1915')],brick='?'),
N('bluff-fort','Bluff Fort','Bluff','ut','fort','standing',37.28389,-109.55282,'exact','open','drive','forts','1880',
  'The Hole-in-the-Rock pioneers, Latter-day Saints sent to settle the San Juan, reached Bluff in April 1880 after a six-month, 260-mile wagon journey across canyon country, cutting their way down the Hole-in-the-Rock to the Colorado River. They built a fort of cabins here, with a cooperative store that traded with the Navajo.',
  'One original cabin, the Barton Cabin, and an original Hole-in-the-Rock wagon; the other cabins, the meeting house and the co-op store (now the visitor center, 2013) are replicas built by the Hole in the Rock Foundation. Flooding in Bluff has recently covered 13 of the cabins in water and mud, and the foundation is still repairing them.',
  '',
  'Bluff Fort Historic Site, run by the Hole in the Rock Foundation; free admission. After hours the main entrance stays open in daylight for walking the grounds. Gift shop and bakery closed Sunday.',
  [W('Bluff Fort'),L('Hole in the Rock Foundation: Bluff Fort Historic Site','https://hirf.org/'),L('Bluff, Utah: Bluff Fort','https://bluffutah.org/bluff-fort/')],
  brick='?',hours='Summer 9 a.m. to 6 p.m.; November 1 to February 28, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; grounds open in daylight after hours'),
# ---- southwest ----
N('frisco-charcoal-kilns','Frisco Charcoal Kilns','Frisco','ut','industry','standing',38.46082,-113.2629,'exact','view','drive','','1877–1884',
  'Five granite beehive kilns, survivors of 36 in the San Francisco Mining District, built between 1877 and 1880 by the Frisco Mining and Smelting Company to make charcoal for smelting ore from the Horn Silver Mine. The Horn Silver caved in on February 12 or 13, 1885 (the sources give both days), the company had closed in 1884, and the boom town of Frisco, once said to hold 6,000 people and 23 saloons, emptied.',
  'Five beehive kilns of granite, each a different size, 16 to 32 feet across. The ghost town of Frisco lies round them.',
  'The beehive kilns of the wildest town in the Great Basin.',
  'Off Highway 21 west of Milford. The 1982 Register form gave a mining company as owner, and the federal land map shows no public land here; an archived BLM release describes a BLM survey at the "Frisco Kiln site" and says a high-clearance vehicle is needed. Who owns it now is not settled: look from the road and keep away from old mine workings.',
  [W('Frisco Charcoal Kilns'),W('Frisco, Utah'),L('National Register nomination: Frisco Charcoal Kilns (1982, NPGallery)','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/82004793_text'),
   L('BLM: National Public Lands Day events (archived release)','https://www.blm.gov/press-release/blm-hosts-national-public-lands-day-events')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('silver-reef-wells-fargo','Silver Reef and the Wells Fargo building','Leeds','ut','town','standing',37.25306,-113.36667,'site','open','drive','','c. 1877',
  'Silver was found here in sandstone, where geologists said it could not be, and by 1879 about 2,000 people lived in Silver Reef along a mile-long Main Street. Most mines closed by 1884 as the price of silver fell, and by 1901 most buildings had been pulled down or moved to Leeds. The Wells Fargo office of about 1877, built by the local masons George Brooks and Ira McMullin, survived as a store, a house and a mining office.',
  'The red sandstone Wells Fargo building, ashlar in front and rubble on the other sides, with its vault, now a museum and art gallery; the Rice Building, the Cosmopolitan Restaurant and many foundations and walls; the Catholic and Protestant cemeteries, restored in 1998. New houses now stand among the ruins.',
  'A silver town in a sandstone reef.',
  'The Wells Fargo building is owned by Washington County; 1903 Wells Fargo Road, about a mile west of Leeds. The museum\'s own page would not open; the county\'s page shows it open 10 a.m. to 3 p.m. on the days of a December free-admission week, but no regular hours were read.',
  [W('Silver Reef, Utah'),W('Wells Fargo and Company Express Building'),L('Washington County: Silver Reef Museum notices','https://www.washco.utah.gov/tag/silver-reef')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('grafton-ghost-town','Grafton','Rockville','ut','town','standing',37.16722,-113.08,'site','public','drive','','1859–1944',
  'Settlers sent to grow cotton founded Wheeler on the Virgin River in December 1859; the Great Flood of January 1862 destroyed it and they rebuilt a mile upriver as Grafton. Silt, floods and conflict wore it down, the church branch closed in 1921 and the last residents left in 1944. It was a location for In Old Arizona (1929) and Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid.',
  'The old church, the Russell home, the Louisa Foster home and the John Wood home, restored by the Grafton Heritage Partnership with the BLM and the state; orchards and fields along the river; and the cemetery on the left before the town, with a marker listing deaths of the 1860s. Interior not checked.',
  'Said to be the most photographed ghost town in the West.',
  'Looked after by the Grafton Heritage Partnership, whose page gives the way: from SR 9 past Rockville\'s centre turn south on Bridge Road, cross the river, turn right and keep right, following signs a few miles. Dead-end road with no water, power or toilets; impassable in hard rain. Who owns each parcel not checked.',
  [W('Grafton, Utah'),L('Grafton Heritage Partnership: directions','https://graftonheritage.org/directions/'),
   L('HMdb: Grafton cemetery marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=272712')],brick='?'),
N('brigham-young-winter-home','Brigham Young Winter Home and Office','St. George','ut','landmark','standing',37.11139,-113.585,'exact','open','drive','','1870s',
  'Brigham Young spent his winters in St. George from 1870 to 1877. He bought this house in 1872 and doubled its size, and built the office beside it while the St. George Temple was being finished. After his death it was a private house; the state restored it and opened it in 1963, and the church has run it since 1974.',
  'A two-storey house of beige adobe brick on a red sandstone basement, with fireplaces of red sandstone and adobe; the office has stucco walls and a sandstone chimney. Both are furnished to the 1870s with some of Young\'s own furniture, shown by free guided tours.',
  '',
  'Run by the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints as a historic site open year-round; 67 West 200 North, St. George. Phone +1-435-677-6230.',
  [W('Brigham Young Winter Home and Office'),LDSBY],brick='no',hours='Monday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 6 p.m., Sunday noon to 6'),
]
